Nigeria Maize Yield Forecasting: Deep Learning vs Baseline

A comparative benchmark of Deep Learning and baseline machine-learning approaches for maize yield forecasting in Nigeria using subnational agricultural and environmental data.

Project Overview

This project evaluates whether deep learning models can outperform simple and conventional machine-learning baselines for maize yield forecasting in Nigeria (2000–2023).

Models Evaluated

Persistence Baseline (naive forecast, state-aware), Random Forest (state-aware, hyperparameter-tuned), LSTM, GRU, Bi-LSTM + Attention, Temporal Convolutional Network (TCN), and a Deep Learning Ensemble (average of DL predictions).

All models are trained with walk-forward validation and hyperparameter tuning (random search over 5 trials for DL models, 8 trials for Random Forest). Early stopping is performed on a held-out validation set (last 2 years per state), and the test set is the last 3 years per state.

Data

The project uses HarvestStat Africa subnational maize yield statistics together with environmental predictors, including NDVI, Land Surface Temperature (LST), Precipitation, Temperature, cyclical year encodings (year_sin, year_cos), the state identifier (admin_1, one-hot encoded), and lagged target features (value_lag1, value_lag2).

Key Results

Persistence Baseline achieved R² = 0.7319, MAE = 0.155, RMSE = 0.232, MAPE = 7.10%.

Random Forest (tuned) achieved R² = 0.5793, MAE = 0.226, RMSE = 0.291, MAPE = 10.80%.

Bi-LSTM + Attention (tuned) achieved R² = 0.5343, MAE = 0.232, RMSE = 0.306, MAPE = 11.87%.

LSTM (tuned) achieved R² = 0.4078, MAE = 0.271, RMSE = 0.345, MAPE = 14.53%.

Ensemble (DL avg) achieved R² = 0.3670, MAE = 0.279, RMSE = 0.357, MAPE = 13.33%.

GRU (tuned) achieved R² = 0.2516, MAE = 0.303, RMSE = 0.388, MAPE = 14.68%.

TCN (tuned) achieved R² = -0.5919, MAE = 0.468, RMSE = 0.566, MAPE = 20.48%.

Main Findings

1. State identity is essential.
Without state information, all deep-learning models and Random Forest produced negative R². Adding one-hot state encoding raised Random Forest from R² = -0.17 to R² = +0.51.

2. Lagged target features are the missing link.
Adding value_lag1 and value_lag2 lifted every deep-learning model from negative to positive R². Bi-LSTM + Attention improved from R² = -0.22 to R² = +0.53.

3. The Persistence baseline remains the strongest model, with R² = 0.73. This result demonstrates the importance of evaluating complex forecasting models against strong and simple baselines, particularly when working with limited annual data and high autocorrelation.

4. Complexity does not guarantee accuracy.
Despite hyperparameter tuning, walk-forward validation, and state encoding, deep-learning models did not outperform a well-designed naive baseline.

Reproducibility

Fixed random seed (SEED = 42) across NumPy, Python random, and PyTorch. Three-way walk-forward split (train / validation / test per state) with no leakage. Hyperparameter search performed on the validation set; final models retrained on train + validation. Test set kept untouched until final evaluation.

Technologies

Python (Pandas / NumPy / Scikit-learn / PyTorch / Matplotlib)

Data Citation

Lee, D., Anderson, W., Chen, X., Davenport, F., Shukla, S., Sahajpal, R., Budde, M., Rowland, J., Verdin, J., You, L., Ahouangbenon, M., Davis, K. F., Kebede, E., Ehrmann, S., Justice, C., & Meyer, C. (2025). HarvestStat Africa – Harmonized Subnational Crop Statistics for Sub-Saharan Africa.

